.\"    Man page for pass-securid
.\"
.TH pass-securid 1 "2026-08-11" "1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pass-securid \- generate and store RSA SecurID tokencodes with pass
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pass securid
.RI [ code ]
.RB [ \-\-clip ", " \-c ]
.RB [ \-\-quiet ", " \-q ]
.RB [ \-\-pin =\fIpin\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-time =\fItime\fP ]
.IR pass-name
.PP
.B pass securid
.B insert
.RB [ \-\-force ", " \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-echo ", " \-e ]
.RB [ \-\-store\-seed ", " \-s ]
.RB [ \-\-file =\fIfile\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-pin =\fIpin\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP ]
.RI [ pass-name ]
.PP
.B pass securid
.B append
.RB [ \-\-force ", " \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-echo ", " \-e ]
.RB [ \-\-store\-seed ", " \-s ]
.RB [ \-\-file =\fIfile\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-pin =\fIpin\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP ]
.IR pass-name
.PP
.B pass securid
.B uri
.RB [ \-\-clip ", " \-c ]
.IR pass-name
.PP
.B pass securid
.B info
.RB [ \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP ]
.IR pass-name
.PP
.B pass securid
.B validate
.RB [ \-\-file =\fIfile\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP ]
.RI [ token-string ]
.PP
.B pass securid
.BR version | help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pass-securid
is an extension for the
.B pass
password manager that stores RSA SecurID 128-bit (AES) software tokens in the
password store and generates their current tokencodes.  It requires only
.B bash
and
.B python3
(the Python standard library): parsing, seed decryption, AES, and tokencode
computation are performed by an embedded, dependency-free engine.  The
.I stoken
tool is not required.
.PP
A token is stored as a single line in a password file: a numeric 81\(en104
digit "ctf" string, an iPhone/Android URI, or (from QR scanning) any of
these.  Optional metadata lines cache the PIN, device ID, and decrypted seed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
com.rsa.securid.iphone://ctf?ctfData=...
pin: 1234
devid: a01c4380-fc01-4df0-b113-7fb98ec74694
seed: 0cd1105cd1aafd893e89f1eb7b800dd8
.fi
.RE
.PP
The credentials needed for a particular token are resolved from command-line
flags, cached metadata, or an interactive prompt, in that order.  Tokens that
are not bound to a password or device ID therefore produce a code with no
interaction at all.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B code
Print (or copy to the clipboard with
.BR \-\-clip )
the current tokencode for
.IR pass-name .
This is the default command:
.B pass securid pass-name
is equivalent to
.BR "pass securid code pass-name" .
.TP
.B insert
Prompt for a SecurID token string (or accept it as the positional argument,
via
.BR \-\-file ,
or from standard input) and store it in the vault.  The token is validated
before it is written.  If the token is bound to a device ID and none is
given, it is prompted for once and cached.  With
.BR \-\-store-seed ,
the token is decrypted (prompting for the password if needed) and the
decrypted seed is cached so later codes require no password.  Without
.BR \-\-store-seed ,
a password-protected token keeps asking for its password on each code.
.TP
.B append
Attach a SecurID token to an existing password entry, replacing a token line
if one is already present.
.TP
.B uri
Print the stored token string for
.IR pass-name .
.TP
.B info
Print the token's serial number, key length, digit count, interval,
expiration date, and protection flags.  Password/device ID are prompted for
when the token requires them.
.TP
.B validate
Check that a token string is well-formed and decryptable; print its serial
number on success.
.TP
.B version
Print the extension version.
.TP
.B help
Show usage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-clip
Send the generated code (or token URI) to the clipboard instead of standard
output.  The clipboard is cleared after
.B $CLIP_TIME
seconds.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print the code without a trailing newline (scripting convenience).
.TP
.B \-\-pin =\fIpin\fP
Supply the token PIN.
.TP
.B \-\-password =\fIpassword\fP
Supply the token decryption password.
.TP
.B \-\-devid =\fIdevid\fP
Supply the token's device ID.
.TP
.B \-\-time =\fItime\fP
Compute the code for a specific time: a unix timestamp, or a relative offset
such as
.B +60
or
.BR \-30 .
Primarily a testing aid.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing password entry without prompting.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-echo
Echo the token string while prompting for it.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store-seed
During
.B insert
or
.BR append ,
decrypt the seed now and cache it as a
.B seed:
line in the password file.
.TP
.B \-\-file =\fIfile\fP
Read the token string from
.I file
(or
.B \-
for standard input) instead of prompting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $PREFIX/.extensions/securid.bash
Extension location, or the directory named by
.BR $PASSWORD_STORE_EXTENSIONS_DIR .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PASSWORD_STORE_SECURID_PYTHON
Path to the
.B python3
interpreter to use (default:
.BR python3 ).
.TP
.B PASSWORD_STORE_ENABLE_EXTENSIONS
Must be
.B true
for pass to load extensions.
.TP
.B PASSWORD_STORE_EXTENSIONS_DIR
Directory containing the
.B securid.bash
extension.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Software tokens are easy to replicate; anyone with the seed can generate
valid tokencodes.  The pass vault's encryption is the security boundary:
everything, including the optional cached seed, PIN, and device ID, is
stored inside gpg/age-encrypted password files.  Storing the decrypted seed
(
.BR \-\-store-seed )
relaxes the token's own password to "the pass vault password"; do not use it
if you need defense-in-depth beyond pass itself.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B sdtid
XML files are not parsed; convert them first (e.g.
.BR "stoken export --blocks" ).
This extension is not affiliated with RSA Security; it implements the
publicly documented SecurID 128-bit (AES) token algorithm.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pass (1),
.BR pass-otp (1),
.BR stoken (1)
.SH AUTHOR
pass-securid, a pass extension modeled on pass-otp.
.PP
